    "description": "The OrderWorkflow begins with a customer identifier, loads the CustomerProfile, derives the shipping region from that profile, uses the region to request a DeliveryQuote, and then attempts to reserve inventory. Each stage may return no value, fail with a domain error, or complete asynchronously. Later stages cannot begin until an earlier result exists, and the workflow must stop at the first domain failure while translating that failure into an HTTP response. The current implementation contains nullable checks, try/catch blocks, and nested callbacks around every transition. The desired solution should keep the order of business steps visible, propagate absent and failed outcomes consistently, and allow the production handler to await the entire computation while tests substitute deterministic outcomes. Decide how to structure this dependent sequence so effect handling does not leak into the business rules and does not accidentally serialize unrelated work.",
    "decision_statement": "Decide how to compose a sequence whose next action depends on the prior result while absence, failure, and asynchronous completion are propagated uniformly.",
    "notes": "Monad fits the dependent stage sequence with uniform absence, failure and async propagation; interpreter-as-data is the near-miss (same pipeline executed by production vs deterministic-test interpreters) and functor supports per-stage mapping. Replaced the applicative sibling, which cannot express the required data dependencies, and dropped contravariant-validators from supporting since no cross-type rule reuse is described."
  },
